      "text": "Let $r$ be an integer root of the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0.$ By Vieta's formulas, we know that the sum of the roots is $-3,$ so $r + s + t = -3,$ where $r,$ $s,$ and $t$ are the other two roots of the polynomial.\nWe can rewrite the polynomial as $(x - r)(x^2 + (3 + r)x + (7 + r + sr)).$\nExpanding, we get $x^3 + (3 + r)x^2 + (7 + r + sr)x - r(x^2 + (3 + r)x + (7 + r + sr)).$\nMatching coefficients, we get\n\\begin{align*}\nx^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 &= x^3 + (3 + r)x^2 + (7 + r + sr)x - r(x^2 + (3 + r)x + (7 + r + sr)) \\\\\n&= x^3 + (3 + r)x^2 + (7 + r + sr)x - rx^2 - r(3 + r)x - r(7 + r + sr) \\\\\n&= x^3 + [3 + (r - r)]x^2 + [(7 + r + sr) - r(3 + r)]x - r(7 + r + sr).\n\\end{align*}Comparing coefficients, we have the following equations:\n\\begin{align*}\n3 + r &= a, \\\\\n7 + r + sr &= 3 + r, \\\\\n- r(7 + r + sr) &= 7.\n\\end{align*}From the third equation, we have $- r(7 + r + sr) = 7,$ so $r = -1.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we have $7 - r - sr = 3 - 1,$ so $sr = 3.$\nThe possible integer pairs $(s, t)$ are $(1, 3),$ $(-1, -3),$ $(3, 1),$ and $(-3, -1),$ so the possible integer values of $a$ are $3,$ $-4,$ $-3,$ and $-4.$\nTherefore, the answer is $\\boxed{-4, 3}.$The answer is: -4,3",
