      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let $r$ be an integer root of the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0$. By the factor theorem, we know that $r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7 = 0$ for any integer $r$.\n\nNow, let's express the polynomial as $(x - r)(x^2 + (p+3)x + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r))$ for some integer $p$. Expanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)x - r(x^2 + (p+3)x + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r))\\]\n\\[= x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)x - r^3 - 3r^2 - arp - 7r - r(p+3)x - r(ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)\\]\n\\[= x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)(1 - r) - (r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7)\\]\n\\[= x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)(1 - r) - (r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7)\\]\n\\[= x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)(1 - r) - (r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7)\\]\n\\[= x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)(1 - r) - (r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7)\\]\n\\[= x^3 + (p+3)x^2 + (ar + 7 + r^2 + 3r)(1 - r) - (r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7)\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients, we get $p = -3$. Therefore, we can rewrite the polynomial as:\n\\[x^3 - 3x^2 + (a - 1)x + 7 = 0\\]\nThis implies that $a - 1 = 0 \\Rightarrow a = 1$. Thus, the only integer value of $a$ that makes the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0$ have at least one integer root is $\\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1",
