      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find all integer values of \\( a \\) such that the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) has at least one integer root, we will use the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed in its lowest terms \\( \\frac{p}{q} \\), must have \\( p \\) as a factor of the constant term (here, 7) and \\( q \\) as a factor of the leading coefficient (here, 1). Therefore, the possible integer roots are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 7 \\).\n\nWe will test each of these possible roots by substituting them into the polynomial and solving for \\( a \\).\n\n1. Testing \\( x = 1 \\):\n\\[\n1^3 + 3(1)^2 + a(1) + 7 = 0 \\implies 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 0 \\implies 11 + a = 0 \\implies a = -11\n\\]\nSo, \\( a = -11 \\) is a solution.\n\n2. Testing \\( x = -1 \\):\n\\[\n(-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + a(-1) + 7 = 0 \\implies -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 0 \\implies 9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9\n\\]\nSo, \\( a = 9 \\) is a solution.\n\n3. Testing \\( x = 7 \\):\n\\[\n7^3 + 3(7)^2 + a(7) + 7 = 0 \\implies 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 0 \\implies 497 + 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -497 \\implies a = -71\n\\]\nSo, \\( a = -71 \\) is a solution.\n\n4. Testing \\( x = -7 \\):\n\\[\n(-7)^3 + 3(-7)^2 + a(-7) + 7 = 0 \\implies -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = 0 \\implies -193 - 7a = 0 \\implies -7a = 193 \\implies a = -\\frac{193}{7}\n\\]\nSince \\( a \\) must be an integer, \\( x = -7 \\) is not a root.\n\nFrom the above tests, the integer values of \\( a \\) that make the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) have at least one integer root are \\( -11, 9, \\) and \\( -71 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\n\\]",
