      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find all integer values of \\(a\\) so that the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) has at least one integer root, we start by using the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed in its lowest terms \\(p/q\\), must be a factor of the constant term (7) divided by a factor of the leading coefficient (1). Therefore, the possible integer roots are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 7 \\).\n\nWe will test each of these potential roots to see if they satisfy the polynomial equation.\n\n1. Testing \\(x = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   1^3 + 3(1)^2 + a(1) + 7 = 0 \\implies 1 + 3 + a + 7 = 0 \\implies 11 + a = 0 \\implies a = -11.\n   \\]\n   So, \\(x = 1\\) is a root when \\(a = -11\\).\n\n2. Testing \\(x = -1\\):\n   \\[\n   (-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + a(-1) + 7 = 0 \\implies -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 0 \\implies 9 - a = 0 \\implies a = 9.\n   \\]\n   So, \\(x = -1\\) is a root when \\(a = 9\\).\n\n3. Testing \\(x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   7^3 + 3(7)^2 + a(7) + 7 = 0 \\implies 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 0 \\implies 497 + 7a = 0 \\implies 7a = -497 \\implies a = -71.\n   \\]\n   So, \\(x = 7\\) is a root when \\(a = -71\\).\n\n4. Testing \\(x = -7\\):\n   \\[\n   (-7)^3 + 3(-7)^2 + a(-7) + 7 = 0 \\implies -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = 0 \\implies -199 - 7a = 0 \\implies -7a = 199 \\implies a = -\\frac{199}{7}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a\\) must be an integer, \\(x = -7\\) is not a root when \\(a = -\\frac{199}{7}\\).\n\nFrom the above, the possible integer values of \\(a\\) are \\(-11, 9, -71\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\n\\]",
